{:edb/db-before-t 2438 :edb/db-after-t 2439 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535751 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:33:51.825Z" 13194139535751 true] [17592186124583 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364786 13194139535751 true] [17592186124583 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364793 13194139535751 true] [17592186124585 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364796 13194139535751 true] [17592186364786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364787 13194139535751 true] [17592186364786 1089 "q-101312" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364786 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535751 true] [17592186364786 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-x}{{x}^{2}-3}=$" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364786 1113 #uuid "d17168f1-346a-54bf-af48-01ec1076b631" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364786 1145 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-x}{{x}^{2}-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(x-\\frac{1}{x})}{(1-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }x \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364788 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364789 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364790 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364791 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364792 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1041 "selection" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364789 13194139535751 true] [17592186364787 1110 #uuid "f4a6fd89-9d41-56ea-b077-a1b6e540ea67" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364788 1034 "\\infty " 13194139535751 true] [17592186364788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535751 true] [17592186364788 1111 #uuid "e25f3813-a022-50c9-8183-a17baa4e9675" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364789 1034 "-\\infty " 13194139535751 true] [17592186364789 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535751 true] [17592186364789 1111 #uuid "7ee7260f-e17b-5bc2-893c-a10200b99370" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364790 1034 "1" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364790 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535751 true] [17592186364790 1111 #uuid "1f2e15f6-0541-53c7-a247-aea94c19ff8f" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364791 1034 "\\text{DNE}" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535751 true] [17592186364791 1111 #uuid "2443d773-ae12-5d54-bd33-3e04a0444c1f" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364792 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535751 true] [17592186364792 1111 #uuid "1623ef14-6d1f-5c7e-a1eb-f7bdec40a15e" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364793 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364794 13194139535751 true] [17592186364793 1089 "q-302235" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364793 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535751 true] [17592186364793 1100 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{8{x}^{6}+5}{2{x}^{5}-7x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364793 1113 #uuid "46e469c8-b773-5cdd-85cb-ec925f153af7" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364793 1145 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{8{x}^{6}+5}{2{x}^{5}-7x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(8x+\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})}{(2-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x+0}{2-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,4x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364795 13194139535751 true] [17592186364794 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535751 true] [17592186364794 1041 "field-1" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364794 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364795 13194139535751 true] [17592186364794 1110 #uuid "6c699efb-5074-5e44-95c5-8ba8432e111c" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364795 1034 "\\infty" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535751 true] [17592186364795 1111 #uuid "db46589f-c0a8-5332-84cf-eea5dc4af7c6" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364796 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364797 13194139535751 true] [17592186364796 1089 "q-68068" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364796 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535751 true] [17592186364796 1100 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x}.$" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364796 1113 #uuid "f4cb2e8d-afe2-5fca-a220-99b0b541a3cf" 13194139535751 true] [17592186364796 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{(\\frac{5x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x})}{5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139535751 true] [17592186364797 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364798 13194139535751 true] [17592186364797 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364799 13194139535751 true] [17592186364797 1000 #edb/ref 17